Hi -- this one had me puzzled for awhile. But, short version:

# Doesn't work:

states_list<-areas_list_to_states_list_new(areas = c("K", "O", "M", "H"))

# Works
states_list<-rcpp_areas_list_to_states_list(areas = c("K", "O", "M", "H"))
states_list

# Longer but lamer also works
states_list<-areas_list_to_states_list_new(areas = c(0,1,2,3))
# Fix states_lists with "_" instead of NA for null range
if (is.null(states_list) == FALSE)
{
if (is.na <http://is.na>(states_list[[1]]) == FALSE)
{
if (states_list[[1]] == "_")
{
states_list[[1]] = NA
} # END if (states_list[[1]] == "_")
} # END if (is.na <http://is.na>(states_list[[1]]) == FALSE)
} # END if (is.null(states_list) == FALSE)

Modern BioGeoBEARS wants the actual states_list used internally to be a list of lists of area numbers, not area letters, this is what rcpp_areas_list_to_states_list(). This allows it to go fast in C++ (Rcpp). The areas_list_to_states_list_new() function is mostly to provide human-readable lists of areas, it will work sometimes internally but not for everything.

Cheers,
Nick

